  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "How Gurmukhi works",
      "body": "<p>Gurmukhi was standardised in the 1500s by the second Sikh Guru, and its name means 'from the mouth of the Guru'. It is an <i>abugida</i>: each consonant includes a short a, so <span lang=\"pa\">ਕ</span> is ka. Other vowels are signs written around the consonant.</p>\n<p>Unlike Devanagari, Gurmukhi has no separate letters for vowels at the start of a word. Instead it uses three carriers, <span lang=\"pa\">ੳ ਅ ੲ</span>, and puts the vowel sign on them: <span lang=\"pa\">ਉ</span> is ੳ with the u sign, <span lang=\"pa\">ਇ</span> is ੲ with the i sign.</p>\n<p>Letters hang from a headline that runs across the word: <span lang=\"pa\">ਪੰਜਾਬ</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Tones",
      "body": "<p>Punjabi is the only major Indian language with tones, and the script shows them in an unexpected way. The letters <span lang=\"pa\">ਘ ਝ ਢ ਧ ਭ</span> once stood for breathy sounds. Today, at the start of a word, they are said like <span lang=\"pa\">ਕ ਚ ਟ ਤ ਪ</span> with a low tone that rises again:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"pa\">ਘਰ</td><td>ghar on paper, said kàr (house)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"pa\">ਕਰ</td><td>kar (do), level tone</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Inside or at the end of a word, the same letters, and ਹ, give a high falling tone to the vowel before them instead: <span lang=\"pa\">ਚਾਹ</span> (tea) and <span lang=\"pa\">ਪੜ੍ਹ</span> (read). Listen to the recordings to hear the pitch.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Nasal and double signs",
      "body": "<p>Three small signs above the line are worth learning early.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"pa\">ੰ tippi</td><td>an n or m sound, used with short vowels: <span lang=\"pa\">ਪੰਜ</span> (five)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"pa\">ਂ bindi</td><td>a nasal vowel, used with long vowels: <span lang=\"pa\">ਮਾਂ</span> (mother)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"pa\">ੱ addak</td><td>doubles the consonant after it: <span lang=\"pa\">ਪੱਤਾ</span> (leaf)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Gurmukhi rarely stacks consonants. Only ਰ, ਹ and ਵ regularly attach below another letter, as in <span lang=\"pa\">ਪ੍ਰੀਤ</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "One language, two scripts",
      "body": "<p>Punjabi is written in Gurmukhi in India and in Shahmukhi, a form of the Arabic script, in Pakistan. The spoken language is largely the same across the border.</p>\n<p>The dotted letters (<span lang=\"pa\">ਸ਼ ਖ਼ ਗ਼ ਜ਼ ਫ਼</span>) were added to write words from Persian and Arabic. Many speakers drop the dot in everyday writing and say ਜ for ਜ਼, so you will see both spellings.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
